(p/'learner-only.md').write_text('''# Make the milkshake slower?\n\nStatus: Draft.\n\nYou run a fast-food chain. Make your morning shake worth coming back for. Easier to sip, or longer to finish?\n\nFirst instinct · unscored. Full cup at the shop.\n\n- I make it thinner.\n- I make it thicker.\n\n## After the concept: afternoon\n\nImagined afternoon: a parent buys a shake after lunch. The child takes a few slow sips, then goes back to chatting. Half leaves on the tray.\n\n- I put two samples on the counter.\n\nYou can offer less shake with the same slow sips, or the full amount with quicker sips. Which afternoon sample do you put on the menu?\n\nImagined sample rules: smaller cup, same recipe, fewer slow sips. Full cup, thinner recipe, more quick sips. Both can be finished alongside the meal.\n\nWhat you saw: lunch already eaten; a few slow sips, then back to chatting.\n\n- I launch the smaller, thick shake.\n- I launch the full-size, thinner shake.\n''')
(p/'author-notes.md').write_text('''# Bounded milkshake repair 2\n\nStatus: Draft. Human newcomer tests: 0.\n\nThe rejected first version is archived in first-repair/. This is one bounded mechanism change following a NONE cold gate, not wording polish of checkout. First source discovery remains. The second occasion is now an imagined meal; the customer consumes a little after food and resumes conversation. Two proposed changes both solve finishing within the meal, but preserve different things: a few slow sips versus the full quantity. That requires inferring which experience may matter from observed use; an explicit desire for speed no longer selects the answer.\n\nStill two meaningful decisions. The sample-reveal button is a continuation, not counted as agency. No free-text input, score, invented sales or satisfaction outcome. Choosing smaller suggests a better-supported hypothesis from this observation; the deck explicitly does not establish family preference or repeat purchase. The larger thinner offer protects quantity, and remains a plausible offer for a different need. Its branch notices the gap and provides a comparison, not a failure grade.\n\nSource: coordinator-verified HBS Online https://online.hbs.edu/blog/post/jobs-to-be-done-examples and local jtbd-premises/sources.md. The first case’s purchase timing, single-item orders, commute hunger/boredom and one-handed consumption are source-grounded. Thicker consistency is a recommendation, not demonstrated sales causation.\n\nAll afternoon behaviour, meal setting, sample quantities and timing equivalence are imagined. Rules appear before the second decision. Small-cup completion is an illustrated possibility, not a guaranteed customer response. The thinner branch makes no claim the child drinks more. The concluding small-treat interpretation is a hypothesis, not mind-reading. No testimonials attributed to real customers. The visible comparison retains tray, food plate, cup and straw. Approved embedded fox unchanged; reduced motion retains all labelled states.\n\nRisk: still only two product buttons, with a fairly strong smaller-serving cue from leftovers. A critic may fairly judge it a quiz with softer grading, or too hedged to create satisfying stakes. This is the last repair of that blocker; if cold play still returns NONE, reject this deck instead of adding more explanatory lines. No claim of fun, learning or transfer. Browser review reserved for coordinator/reviewer; author checked structure and syntax only.\n\nNext step: cold play review of this revision.\n''')
